\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the complexity of computing the VC Dimension and Littlestone's Dimension.
Given an explicit description of a finite universe and a concept class (a binary matrix whose $(x,C)$-th entry is $1$ iff element $x$ belongs to concept $C$),
both can be computed exactly in quasi-polynomial time ($n^{O(\log n)}$).
Assuming the randomized Exponential Time Hypothesis (ETH), we prove nearly matching lower bounds on the running time, that hold even for {\em approximation} algorithms.
\end{abstract}

